---
// src/pages/graphic-engine.astro
// Technical breakdown of the XEMA custom C++ engine.

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="XEMA Engine · Technical Breakdown — Fernando Fernández Andúeza"
  description="Technical architecture of XEMA — a custom C++ game engine with OpenGL 4.5, ECS, Wave Function Collapse, and a Nintendo Switch port."
>
  <div class="container">

    <!-- ── PAGE HEADER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="page-header">
      <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to portfolio</a>

      <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:.875rem; margin-bottom:.875rem; flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <span class="badge badge-personal"><span class="badge-dot"></span>Personal project</span>
        <span class="card-engine">C++ &nbsp;·&nbsp; OpenGL 4.5 &nbsp;·&nbsp; PC &nbsp;·&nbsp; Nintendo Switch</span>
      </div>

      <h1>XEMA — Custom 3D Engine</h1>
      <p class="page-intro">
        A C++ engine built from scratch, without relying on an existing framework, to work
        through every layer of a 3D pipeline directly — rendering, memory, threading, and
        cross-platform portability. Shipped to Nintendo Switch; a PS4 port is currently
        in progress.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── CORE SYSTEMS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2>Core systems</h2>
      <div class="tech-grid">

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Entity Component System</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Components stored in contiguous arrays, with <strong>O(1) access</strong> via
              ID-based entity management instead of map lookups on the hot path.
            </li>
            <li>
              Parent-child hierarchy built directly into the component arrays,
              no separate scene graph structure.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Job System</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Thread pool built on <strong>std::future</strong> for fire-and-forget async work.
            </li>
            <li>
              Used for asynchronous model loading and to keep procedural generation
              off the main thread, so neither one stalls the render loop.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Procedural generation</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Wave Function Collapse</strong>, generating both biomes and
              transitional biomes between them.
            </li>
            <li>
              Runs multithreaded, followed by a separate decoration pass that places
              detail objects once the layout is resolved.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── GRAPHICS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2>Graphics &amp; rendering pipeline</h2>
      <div class="tech-grid">

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>OpenGL 4.5 DSA pipeline</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Built on <strong>Direct State Access</strong>, avoiding the redundant
              bind calls that older-style OpenGL code tends to accumulate.
            </li>
            <li>
              Custom vertex buffer and VAO management, instanced rendering, UBO-based
              uniform blocks, and hand-written GLSL shaders for each stage.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Rendering techniques</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Deferred shading</strong> — a G-Buffer pass stores position,
              normal, albedo, and material data.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>PCF shadow mapping</strong> for softer shadow edges, and a
              <strong>PBR</strong> metallic/roughness workflow using the Cook-Torrance BRDF.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Platform abstraction</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Platform differences are resolved at <strong>link-time</strong>, not
              through virtual dispatch, so the render path stays branch-free.
            </li>
            <li>
              One rendering interface switches between OpenGL on PC and EGL on Switch
              without any changes in the layers above it.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── CROSS-PLATFORM PORTS ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2>Cross-platform ports</h2>
      <div class="tech-grid">

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Nintendo Switch — shipped</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Ported through <strong>libnx</strong> and <strong>EGL</strong> for the
              OpenGL context on Switch hardware.
            </li>
            <li>
              Supports docked (1080p) and handheld (720p) modes, switching resolution
              automatically.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>PlayStation 4 — in progress</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Migrating the engine from OpenGL to 
              <strong> GNM/GNMX </strong> graphics API.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Build system</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Premake5</strong> generates platform-specific build files from
              one Lua script, integrated with the devkitPro Makefile for Switch.
            </li>
            <li>
              No engine fork — the same C++ source compiles for every target.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── ADDITIONAL SYSTEMS ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2>Additional systems</h2>
      <div class="tech-grid">

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Physics &amp; scripting</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>PhysX 5</strong>, through a custom Physics Manager that owns
              the scene and dispatches simulation steps.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Lua scripting</strong> via sol2, exposing engine types to Lua
              without hand-written binding boilerplate.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="padding-bottom: 5rem;"></div>

  </div><!-- /.container -->

</Layout>